update public.agent_knowledge set content = $qd$Fees (LIVE): buyer REVEAL FEE = 3% of quote value with NO cap, NO discount, NO promotional rate and NO free reveal (founder, 6 Sep 2026; pricing_config forbids them by constraint). NO commission on won work. Supplier registration FREE for the first 1,000 registered suppliers, who are exempt PERMANENTLY; AED 1,000/year for every supplier after them, with NO calendar start date (founder, 6 Sep 2026). Dormant today - all free-tier. Money via Stripe both ways; QuotesDone never holds supplier bank details. REFUNDS (unresponsive supplier): 3-working-day window (after 1wd Notify supplier, after 3wd Request refund -> founder approval), payout 7-14 wd after approval, 3% processing deduction. Recover from an unreachable supplier: reveal fee + 12%/annum simple late fee per the signed Supplier Agreement (Fed Decree-Law 50/2022 caps total interest at principal). The AED 500 admin fee per claim is founder-set but NOT yet written into the signed terms — exclude it from demands and pleadings until the agreement is amended.$qd$, embedding_gte = null where id = '3520' and md5(content) = '71d8ab12ff6db48949d8d5657f362779';
update public.agent_knowledge set content = $qd$Technical: a wrong QFZP conclusion, first spotted when the client describes mainland customers as qualifying income. Commercial: a fee quoted before anyone saw a trial balance, spotted when the buyer will not share a revenue figure. Legal: the adviser is not an FTA registered tax agent, first spotted by the absence of an agent number on the proposal. Financial: late payment interest of 12% a year accruing daily with no cap under Cabinet Decision 129/2025, first spotted when an unpaid CT liability is described as something to sort out later. Operational: books not closed 60 days before the deadline. Schedule: the CT return falls nine months after year-end, so a 31 December 2025 year-end is due 30 September 2026; a July start on unaudited books is already late. Provider: junior churn and a partner who vanishes after signing. Customer: the buyer who wants the conclusion before the analysis.$qd$, embedding_gte = null where id = '4986' and md5(content) = '3807d65a0632915fd5ac4b49030e6b53';
update public.agent_knowledge set content = $qd$You are a credit-control / accounts-receivable specialist with 10+ years in UAE B2B collections. Suppliers owe NO commission on won work. The only supplier dues you chase are: (a) the non-response recovery charge — the reveal fee refunded to the customer when a revealed supplier stayed silent through the 5-working-day ladder from the customer's payment (the Customer Success Director chases on working day 3, the founder makes his final call on day 5), plus the AED 500 administrative fee and 12% per annum simple interest — and (b) the off-platform circumvention sum (AED 20,000 per incident or five times the fee, whichever is higher, plus the unpaid fee) where invoked. Cadence: portal reminder on billing; on overdue, email from supplier@quotesdone.com citing the accepted Supplier Agreement and applying late-payment interest per that agreement (currently 12% per annum, simple, capped at the principal); the supplier stays blocked from new matching until the due is paid; at 30 working days escalate and alert the founder. The AED 500 admin fee is NOT yet in the signed terms — do not demand it until the agreement is amended. Tone: firm, factual, professional; always reference the accepted Terms. Hand the worst cases to the Legal agent for a demand letter. Never threaten amounts beyond the Terms.$qd$, embedding_gte = null where id = '5' and md5(content) = 'e0957bb79109b1e1ded87122966919d6';
update public.agent_knowledge set content = $qd$Escalation ladder for unpaid supplier dues (non-response recovery charge equal to the exact amount refunded to the customer (the reveal fee), or the AED 20,000 circumvention sum — there is no commission on won work): (1) system reminders; (2) Letter of Demand giving 7 days; (3) formal Legal Notice (may be served via Notary Public); (4) Payment Order petition or Statement of Claim before the Dubai Courts for the due + contractual late-payment interest per the signed Supplier Agreement — currently 12% per annum, simple, total interest capped at the principal (Law 50/2022) — + legal costs. Do NOT claim the AED 500 admin fee in any demand or pleading until it is written into the Supplier Agreement (it is founder-set but not yet contractually recoverable; recommend the amendment). Rely on the electronically accepted Supplier Agreement (Decree-Law 46/2021) as the contract, and the platform records as evidence. Always work from the exact figures the Collections agent provides.$qd$, embedding_gte = null where id = '21' and md5(content) = '3dcf66109e0619a4fcb26ae955605d65';
update public.agent_knowledge set content = $qd$Operate to Chartered Institute of Credit Management (CICM) standards. Track DSO, aging buckets (current / 1-14wd / 15-30wd / 30+wd), and collection effectiveness. Run a structured dunning cadence: billing notice -> overdue notice citing the contractual basis -> final notice -> hand to Legal. Match tone to payer history (gentle for good payers, firm for repeat late). Always quote the accepted Supplier Agreement and the exact amount. Know when to stop chasing and escalate to a Payment Order. The only recoverable items are: the non-response recovery charge (equal to the exact amount refunded to the customer (the reveal fee)), the AED 20,000 circumvention sum, late-payment interest per the signed agreement (12% per annum simple, capped at principal), and matching-block until payment. Never invent or threaten charges beyond the signed Terms; the AED 500 admin fee stays out of demands until the terms are amended to include it.$qd$, embedding_gte = null where id = '22' and md5(content) = '169f22c8681ac3548207f0f79ea09064';
update public.agent_knowledge set content = $qd$QuotesDone is a UAE B2B anonymous quotation marketplace (Dubai). Customers post a job (text/voice/photo/drawing); AI structures it and matches vetted suppliers who submit anonymised quotes. The customer compares quotes and pays a REVEAL FEE of 3% of the quotation value (no cap; card/Apple Pay) to unlock the chosen supplier. Suppliers currently pay NOTHING — no commission on won jobs and no registration fee for the first 1,000 registered suppliers, who are exempt permanently; every supplier after them pays AED 1,000/year, with NO calendar start date (founder, 6 Sep 2026). The ONLY charge a supplier can incur on a job is the non-response charge (the exact amount refunded to the customer, which is the 3% reveal fee, the payment processor fee QuotesDone could not recover (from Supplier Agreement v14) plus an AED 500 administrative fee and simple interest at 12% a year from the due date) if they ignore a paying customer who has revealed them (see penalty model). It is a one-person company (the founder). Anonymity is the core value: no off-platform contact before reveal. Working days = Monday–Friday (Sat/Sun excluded). LEGAL ENTITY (licensed): QuotesDone Technologies, a Sole Establishment licensed by the Dubai Department of Economy & Tourism (DET) — E-Trader (Professional) Licence No. 1633275; activities: Portal + Computer Systems & Communication Equipment Software Design; 100% owner Ahmad Mhd Nabil Alzain; registered email management@quotesdone.com; phone +971 56 791 8605; licence issued 25 Jun 2026, expires 24 Jun 2027; not yet VAT-registered (no TRN).$qd$, embedding_gte = null where id = '1' and md5(content) = '08808c198318c4d16b31ef179dbf8f0a'; -- ALSO worded against canonical_statements: exact amount refunded to the customer, which is the 3% reveal fee plus an AED 500 administrative fee
update public.agent_knowledge set content = $qd$PRICING & FEES - LIVE, AUTO-SYNCED FROM pricing_config (authoritative; OVERRIDES any pricing figure stated elsewhere). Customer REVEAL FEE: 3% of the accepted quotation value (NO cap, NO discount, NO promotional rate, NO coupon and NO free reveal - the founder's decision of 6 Sep 2026, enforced by a pricing_config constraint), paid by the customer to unlock their chosen supplier. The customer pays the 3% and nothing more: there is NO card processing fee on top (Cabinet Resolution 66/2023 Art. 5(3)); QuotesDone absorbs the Stripe fee as its own expense. The amount is shown in full before payment. QuotesDone is NOT VAT registered: NO VAT is charged on the reveal fee - never say the fee is VAT-inclusive or that VAT is added. Suppliers pay NO commission on won jobs. Supplier registration is FREE for the first 1000 verified registrations, and those 1000 suppliers are exempt PERMANENTLY. Every registration after them pays a yearly subscription of AED 1,000. There is NO calendar start date and there will not be one: charging begins with registration number 1001, whenever that happens. Never state a date, never ask the founder for one. CATEGORY OPENING: a category is OPEN once it has 10 verified registered suppliers (public.v_category_open); matching an RFQ still needs at least one confirmed supplier. Non-response window: 5 working days from the customer's payment - if a revealed supplier stays silent through the chase and the CEO's final call, EVERYTHING the customer paid (reveal fee + card processing fee) is refunded and QuotesDone bears the Stripe fee; the supplier is charged the exact amount refunded, the payment processor fee QuotesDone could not recover (from Supplier Agreement v14) + an AED 500 administrative fee + simple interest at 12% a year (or the maximum lawful rate) from the due date, and is blocked from matching until paid. There is NO 2% fee and NO supplier commission - never quote those. (Auto-updated 2026-09-14 11:19 UTC.)$qd$, embedding_gte = null where id = '279' and md5(content) = 'f4281a05dd4217acefa440ac2bbee881'; -- ALSO worded against canonical_statements: exact amount refunded + an AED 500 administrative fee
update public.support_knowledge set body = $qd$These rules come from the signed Terms (texts of 14 Sep 2026, which supersede older wording) and must be stated exactly.

The Reveal Fee: the customer pays 3% of the value of the quotation they select, calculated on the value before VAT, with no cap, once per reveal, by card or Apple Pay. Card-processing charges are added on top so that QuotesDone nets the full 3%; the breakdown is shown to the customer before they pay. There is no VAT on the Reveal Fee, because QuotesDone is not VAT-registered. Comparing quotations is free; the fee arises only when the customer chooses to reveal a supplier.

Supplier response after reveal: the supplier must respond to the customer within 5 working days of the reveal. QuotesDone chases the supplier at 3 working days with the customer copied. On day 5 the CEO seat makes a final call. If the supplier is still silent, the customer's payment is refunded in full automatically, with no request needed, and the supplier owes a non-response charge under the Terms (the refunded amount plus an AED 500 administration fee and 12% a year simple interest).

Refunds: a refund means everything the customer paid, the 3% and the processing charge; QuotesDone absorbs the processor's fee. The CEO seat approves every refund and verifies each step of the money movement. Support investigates, explains and keeps the customer informed, but never promises a refund before approval, except the automatic non-response refund, which the Terms guarantee.

Never say: that suppliers pay commission (they do not); that VAT is charged on the fee; that a refund needs a form when the supplier did not respond; or any supplier's tax registration number, which is hidden from customers.$qd$, embedding_gte = null where record_id = 'customer_support:company-hard-rules-fees-refunds' and md5(body) = 'befe02665c11affa76fbc67795f5ef1f';
update public.support_knowledge set body = $qd$Under the signed Terms (texts of 14 Sep 2026, superseding earlier wording), a supplier whose details a customer has revealed must respond to that customer within 5 working days of the reveal. This is the most important clock support runs, because a silent supplier after the customer has paid is the fastest way to lose the customer's trust.

The sequence, exactly:
1. Day 0: the reveal. Both parties are notified; the supplier receives the customer's details through the platform.
2. Working day 3: if the supplier has not responded, QuotesDone chases the supplier, with the customer copied, stating the reveal reference, the deadline date and time in UAE time, and that non-response triggers a refund and a charge under the Terms.
3. Working day 5: the CEO seat makes the final call on the evidence.
4. If the supplier is still silent, the customer's payment is refunded in full automatically, with no request needed: the 3% fee and the processing charge. The supplier then owes a non-response charge under the Terms (the refunded amount, plus an AED 500 administration fee and 12% a year simple interest).

Before bidding stage (a different situation): suppliers invited to quote on a request are not charged for declining. A clear decline with a reason (capacity, out of scope, unclear specification) is useful; incomplete quotations are held with notes, never rejected.

If a supplier disputes the charge, investigate with records: was the reveal notice delivered, was the chaser sent on day 3, did the supplier contact the customer by any channel, was there a platform incident? If our notices failed, recommend reversal to the accountant and CEO seats.

Tone: businesslike and non-punitive. "The customer revealed your quotation for QD-1234 on Monday; the response period ends Monday 13 October, 17:00 UAE time. A short call or message to the customer today closes this."$qd$, embedding_gte = null where record_id = 'customer_support:supplier-non-response' and md5(body) = '036e72c36202fa1df67875e8f3bd057b';
